1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:00,730
Session four.

2
00:00:00,730 --> 00:00:02,809
nginx, and the code that explains it.

3
00:00:02,809 --> 00:00:07,530
Every design in this session was someone's answer to a constraint that may no longer

4
00:00:07,530 --> 00:00:07,867
exist.

5
00:00:07,867 --> 00:00:10,901
Knowing which constraint tells you a law from a habit.

6
00:00:11,800 --> 00:00:13,042
First, a correction.

7
00:00:13,042 --> 00:00:18,200
The myth says: Berners-Lee wrote the first server, called Mosaic, and Mosaic became

8
00:00:18,200 --> 00:00:18,635
Apache.

9
00:00:18,635 --> 00:00:19,505
That is wrong.

10
00:00:19,505 --> 00:00:21,680
There were three separate programs.

11
00:00:22,000 --> 00:00:26,809
CERN httpd, by Tim Berners-Lee on a NeXT machine in nineteen ninety.

12
00:00:26,809 --> 00:00:28,931
That was the first web server.

13
00:00:29,266 --> 00:00:31,797
NCSA Mosaic, in nineteen ninety-three.

14
00:00:31,797 --> 00:00:33,862
It was a browser, not a server.

15
00:00:33,862 --> 00:00:37,126
It had inline images and took the web mainstream.

16
00:00:37,466 --> 00:00:42,199
NCSA HTTPd, also nineteen ninety-three, by Rob McCool.

17
00:00:42,199 --> 00:00:45,616
That was a server, and it invented CGI.

18
00:00:45,933 --> 00:00:50,837
In nineteen ninety-four McCool left, development stalled, and webmasters traded

19
00:00:50,837 --> 00:00:51,831
patches by mail.

20
00:00:52,133 --> 00:00:56,045
In nineteen ninety-five that patch set continued as a project.

21
00:00:56,045 --> 00:00:57,055
A patchy server.

22
00:00:57,055 --> 00:00:57,497
Apache.

23
00:00:57,800 --> 00:01:03,685
In nineteen ninety-six, Apache passed NCSA HTTPd, and stayed number one for twenty

24
00:01:03,685 --> 00:01:04,115
years.

25
00:01:05,000 --> 00:01:08,053
Apache then spent a decade trying to outrun fork.

26
00:01:08,400 --> 00:01:10,309
Apache one point three, prefork.

27
00:01:10,309 --> 00:01:13,950
A pool of processes forked at startup instead of per request.

28
00:01:13,950 --> 00:01:16,277
The setup cost leaves the request path.

29
00:01:16,277 --> 00:01:19,023
But one process still parks on one connection.

30
00:01:19,333 --> 00:01:21,516
Apache two point zero, the worker MPM.

31
00:01:21,516 --> 00:01:25,939
Processes running many threads, with eight megabytes shared and a stack each.

32
00:01:25,939 --> 00:01:30,420
Linux pthreads in nineteen ninety-nine to two thousand two were new and buggy.

33
00:01:30,733 --> 00:01:32,936
Apache two point four, the event MPM.

34
00:01:32,936 --> 00:01:37,817
A listener thread parks idle keep-alives on epoll, and hands a worker a connection

35
00:01:37,817 --> 00:01:39,127
only when it has work.

36
00:01:39,127 --> 00:01:41,925
It is the default on Unix since two point four.

37
00:01:42,266 --> 00:01:46,869
Three architectures, one question: how do I make the per-connection worker cheaper?

38
00:01:46,869 --> 00:01:48,977
None of them was a different question.

39
00:01:49,866 --> 00:01:53,756
Java's answer was different in style: stop creating things.

40
00:01:54,066 --> 00:01:59,694
CGI, from nineteen ninety-three, did all this for every request: fork, exec, load

41
00:01:59,694 --> 00:02:02,820
the interpreter, parse the script, run, exit.

42
00:02:03,133 --> 00:02:08,255
The servlet, from nineteen ninety-seven, loads the class at startup and instantiates

43
00:02:08,255 --> 00:02:08,743
it once.

44
00:02:08,743 --> 00:02:12,890
Then each request is just a method call: service, request, response.

45
00:02:12,890 --> 00:02:14,841
The object outlives the request.

46
00:02:14,841 --> 00:02:15,939
That is the trick.

47
00:02:16,266 --> 00:02:22,412
J two EE, in nineteen ninety-nine, made this a multi-layer app server: a web tier,

48
00:02:22,412 --> 00:02:24,660
an EJB tier, and the database.

49
00:02:24,999 --> 00:02:28,818
Connection pools do the same thing for the database handshake.

50
00:02:28,818 --> 00:02:31,466
It is the same move as prefork and FastCGI.

51
00:02:32,399 --> 00:02:33,993
Now it is two thousand two.

52
00:02:33,993 --> 00:02:36,649
Here are the numbers everyone was staring at.

53
00:02:36,933 --> 00:02:39,224
Ten thousand concurrent connections.

54
00:02:39,224 --> 00:02:42,343
That is C ten K, Dan Kegel, nineteen ninety-nine.

55
00:02:42,666 --> 00:02:44,800
Eight megabytes of stack per thread.

56
00:02:44,800 --> 00:02:48,239
Ten thousand threads is eighty gigabytes of address space.

57
00:02:48,533 --> 00:02:52,342
And one thousand twenty-four, FD_SETSIZE, hard-coded.

58
00:02:52,342 --> 00:02:54,210
A ulimit does not move it.

59
00:02:54,533 --> 00:02:56,452
Everyone is optimising the worker.

60
00:02:56,452 --> 00:02:58,202
Somebody is about to delete it.

61
00:02:59,133 --> 00:03:03,649
Igor Sysoev, at Rambler, the big Russian portal, in two thousand two.

62
00:03:03,933 --> 00:03:08,423
Rambler's Apache servers were falling over under connection volume, not request

63
00:03:08,423 --> 00:03:08,821
volume.

64
00:03:09,133 --> 00:03:12,497
He started nginx in two thousand two to solve C ten K.

65
00:03:12,497 --> 00:03:17,294
The first public release was on the fourth of October, two thousand four, the

66
00:03:17,294 --> 00:03:18,726
anniversary of Sputnik.

67
00:03:18,726 --> 00:03:22,589
Version one point zero came only in April two thousand eleven.

68
00:03:22,933 --> 00:03:27,835
The goal: serve ten thousand mostly idle connections from one process, and let

69
00:03:27,835 --> 00:03:30,350
something else generate dynamic content.

70
00:03:30,350 --> 00:03:33,869
That is why nginx is a reverse proxy, not an app server.

71
00:03:34,199 --> 00:03:37,058
Apache asked: how do I make the worker cheaper?

72
00:03:37,058 --> 00:03:40,525
nginx asked: why is there a worker per connection at all?

73
00:03:40,525 --> 00:03:45,635
A connection needs a few hundred bytes of state, and a place in a list, until it has

74
00:03:45,635 --> 00:03:46,669
something to say.

75
00:03:47,599 --> 00:03:48,666
The process model.

76
00:03:48,666 --> 00:03:52,696
One process per core, plus a master that never touches a connection.

77
00:03:52,999 --> 00:03:58,095
The master binds port eighty, reads the config, forks, and handles signals.

78
00:03:58,095 --> 00:03:58,979
Nothing else.

79
00:03:59,266 --> 00:04:02,529
Then there is one worker per core, each with its own event loop.

80
00:04:02,866 --> 00:04:07,469
Plus a cache manager, which evicts the least recently used entries, and a cache

81
00:04:07,469 --> 00:04:09,217
loader, which warms the index.

82
00:04:09,533 --> 00:04:11,313
You can check this with ps.

83
00:04:11,313 --> 00:04:16,718
The master has parent process one, and the number of workers does not change under

84
00:04:16,718 --> 00:04:19,553
load, unlike a fork server, where it grows.

85
00:04:19,553 --> 00:04:24,101
The code is ngx_master_process_cycle, ngx_start_worker_processes, and

86
00:04:24,101 --> 00:04:25,288
ngx_spawn_process.

87
00:04:26,199 --> 00:04:28,191
All of nginx, in six lines.

88
00:04:28,191 --> 00:04:30,994
This is ngx_process_events_and_timers.

89
00:04:31,333 --> 00:04:32,812
Find the nearest timer.

90
00:04:32,812 --> 00:04:33,776
Process events.

91
00:04:33,776 --> 00:04:35,641
Process posted accept events.

92
00:04:35,641 --> 00:04:37,249
Release the accept mutex.

93
00:04:37,249 --> 00:04:38,149
Expire timers.

94
00:04:38,149 --> 00:04:40,464
Process the remaining posted events.

95
00:04:40,464 --> 00:04:42,072
That is the whole server.

96
00:04:42,399 --> 00:04:45,402
Notice that the timer is an argument to the sleep.

97
00:04:45,402 --> 00:04:47,444
So nginx never polls for timeouts.

98
00:04:47,444 --> 00:04:50,387
It just sleeps exactly until the next one is due.

99
00:04:50,733 --> 00:04:53,101
And handlers do not run inside the poll.

100
00:04:53,101 --> 00:04:55,470
They are posted, and drained afterwards.

101
00:04:56,399 --> 00:04:58,927
How do the workers share new connections?

102
00:04:58,927 --> 00:04:59,790
With one line.

103
00:05:00,133 --> 00:05:05,602
ngx accept disabled equals the total connection slots divided by eight, minus the

104
00:05:05,602 --> 00:05:06,344
free slots.

105
00:05:06,666 --> 00:05:11,871
In plain words: once I am using more than seven eighths of my connection slots, stop

106
00:05:11,871 --> 00:05:15,650
competing for new connections, and let my siblings take them.

107
00:05:15,650 --> 00:05:17,942
No coordinator and no shared counter.

108
00:05:17,942 --> 00:05:19,553
Each worker decides alone.

109
00:05:19,866 --> 00:05:24,545
The accept mutex has been off by default since version one point eleven point three.

110
00:05:24,866 --> 00:05:29,870
The thundering herd, where all workers wake up for a single connection, was real in

111
00:05:29,870 --> 00:05:30,955
two thousand four.

112
00:05:30,955 --> 00:05:35,777
Now EPOLLEXCLUSIVE, in Linux four point five and later, and SO_REUSEPORT let the

113
00:05:35,777 --> 00:05:37,405
kernel fan connections out.

114
00:05:38,333 --> 00:05:43,360
select versus epoll, stated as: O of watched, versus O of active.

115
00:05:44,399 --> 00:05:46,483
Where does the watch list live?

116
00:05:46,483 --> 00:05:49,977
With select, in your process, rebuilt on every call.

117
00:05:49,977 --> 00:05:53,270
With epoll, in the kernel, edited with epoll_ctl.

118
00:05:53,599 --> 00:05:58,948
The cost per iteration: select does an O of n build, an O of n kernel walk, and an O

119
00:05:58,948 --> 00:05:59,584
of n scan.

120
00:05:59,584 --> 00:06:01,494
epoll costs O of ready events.

121
00:06:01,799 --> 00:06:06,816
With ten thousand idle and ten active, select does thirty thousand units of work to

122
00:06:06,816 --> 00:06:07,360
find ten.

123
00:06:07,360 --> 00:06:08,266
epoll does ten.

124
00:06:08,599 --> 00:06:14,105
The ceiling: FD_SETSIZE of one thousand twenty-four, fixed at compile time, versus

125
00:06:14,105 --> 00:06:14,776
ulimit -n.

126
00:06:15,066 --> 00:06:16,632
And what does ulimit do?

127
00:06:16,632 --> 00:06:20,353
For select, nothing, because the limit is a bitmap width.

128
00:06:20,353 --> 00:06:21,788
For epoll, everything.

129
00:06:22,133 --> 00:06:25,527
select is portable everywhere since nineteen eighty-three.

130
00:06:25,527 --> 00:06:27,868
epoll is Linux only, with kqueue on BSD.

131
00:06:28,199 --> 00:06:32,124
Everything here is downstream of one decision: who owns the watch list.

132
00:06:33,066 --> 00:06:35,910
Every event loop author writes this bug once.

133
00:06:35,910 --> 00:06:36,921
The stale event.

134
00:06:37,266 --> 00:06:39,704
epoll_wait returns a batch of events.

135
00:06:39,999 --> 00:06:43,784
Handling event one may close the connection that event seven points at.

136
00:06:44,133 --> 00:06:48,906
A new client then takes that recycled file descriptor, and event seven is delivered

137
00:06:48,906 --> 00:06:50,286
to the wrong connection.

138
00:06:50,599 --> 00:06:52,008
nginx's fix is clever.

139
00:06:52,008 --> 00:06:57,259
Structures are aligned so the low bit of a pointer is always zero, and that bit is

140
00:06:57,259 --> 00:07:00,012
used as an instance flag, flipped on reuse.

141
00:07:00,012 --> 00:07:01,357
One branch checks it.

142
00:07:01,357 --> 00:07:03,150
A free bit, no extra memory.

143
00:07:03,466 --> 00:07:08,228
The companion repository uses an explicit generation counter instead, which is

144
00:07:08,228 --> 00:07:09,143
easier to read.

145
00:07:10,066 --> 00:07:10,752
sendfile.

146
00:07:10,752 --> 00:07:12,504
Four copies become two.

147
00:07:16,466 --> 00:07:21,256
With read and write, data goes from disk to the page cache, to your buffer, to the

148
00:07:21,256 --> 00:07:23,300
socket buffer, to the network card.

149
00:07:23,300 --> 00:07:25,928
Four copies, and two mode switches per chunk.

150
00:07:29,399 --> 00:07:34,584
With sendfile, data goes from disk to the page cache, to the socket buffer, to the

151
00:07:34,584 --> 00:07:35,406
network card.

152
00:07:35,406 --> 00:07:38,947
Two copies, both by DMA, and no mode switches per chunk.

153
00:07:38,947 --> 00:07:41,160
The data never enters your process.

154
00:07:41,466 --> 00:07:46,719
The limit: if you must touch the bytes, for gzip, TLS or templating, they have to

155
00:07:46,719 --> 00:07:47,952
come to user space.

156
00:07:47,952 --> 00:07:50,805
So gzip on and sendfile on fight each other.

157
00:07:50,805 --> 00:07:52,751
That is why kernel TLS exists.

158
00:07:53,066 --> 00:07:57,554
An honest benchmark, sixty-four megabytes over loopback: read takes thirty-seven

159
00:07:57,554 --> 00:08:00,640
milliseconds and two thousand forty-eight system calls.

160
00:08:00,640 --> 00:08:03,220
mmap takes twenty-three milliseconds and four.

161
00:08:03,220 --> 00:08:06,642
sendfile takes thirty-three milliseconds and one system call.

162
00:08:06,642 --> 00:08:10,962
On loopback there is no network card or DMA, so wall clock is about the same.

163
00:08:10,962 --> 00:08:15,394
The real win is two thousand forty-eight system calls down to one, and headroom

164
00:08:15,394 --> 00:08:16,403
under concurrency.

165
00:08:16,403 --> 00:08:20,442
A benchmark that does not model the bottleneck measures the wrong thing.

166
00:08:21,333 --> 00:08:23,450
Why is it called a reverse proxy?

167
00:08:23,450 --> 00:08:24,990
Because proxy was taken.

168
00:08:25,333 --> 00:08:29,933
A forward proxy, like Squid from nineteen ninety-six, sits next to the clients.

169
00:08:29,933 --> 00:08:32,961
It caches for campus users and protects your uplink.

170
00:08:33,266 --> 00:08:36,783
A reverse proxy, like nginx, sits next to the servers.

171
00:08:36,783 --> 00:08:42,254
It caches, routes by path or host, distributes across upstreams, terminates TLS, and

172
00:08:42,254 --> 00:08:44,338
holds ten thousand slow clients.

173
00:08:44,338 --> 00:08:48,115
The client never configures it, and usually cannot see it.

174
00:08:48,399 --> 00:08:51,817
The killer feature nobody lists: it absorbs slow clients.

175
00:08:51,817 --> 00:08:54,634
Your app never has to talk to a phone on two G.

176
00:08:55,533 --> 00:08:57,595
Now the features you configure.

177
00:08:57,595 --> 00:08:58,726
First, the cache.

178
00:08:59,066 --> 00:09:01,307
One directive defines the cache.

179
00:09:02,333 --> 00:09:07,279
keys zone: ten megabytes of shared memory holding the red-black tree and the LRU

180
00:09:07,279 --> 00:09:07,649
queue.

181
00:09:07,649 --> 00:09:09,628
That is the index, not the data.

182
00:09:09,628 --> 00:09:12,039
About eight thousand keys per megabyte.

183
00:09:12,333 --> 00:09:17,520
levels one colon two: two directory levels on disk, so no directory ever holds a

184
00:09:17,520 --> 00:09:18,428
million files.

185
00:09:18,733 --> 00:09:20,451
max size: the disk ceiling.

186
00:09:20,451 --> 00:09:23,632
The cache manager evicts from the tail of the LRU.

187
00:09:23,933 --> 00:09:29,128
inactive: an item is evicted if it is not read for sixty seconds, regardless of its

188
00:09:29,128 --> 00:09:29,378
TTL.

189
00:09:30,266 --> 00:09:31,365
A class question.

190
00:09:31,365 --> 00:09:35,243
proxy_cache_valid is ten seconds, inactive is sixty seconds.

191
00:09:35,243 --> 00:09:36,083
What happens?

192
00:09:37,400 --> 00:09:39,627
The content is fresh for ten seconds.

193
00:09:39,627 --> 00:09:42,336
After that it is stale, so nginx revalidates.

194
00:09:42,336 --> 00:09:46,490
But it is kept on disk for up to sixty seconds since the last access.

195
00:09:46,800 --> 00:09:51,975
And if nobody reads it for sixty seconds, it is deleted, even if it would still be

196
00:09:51,975 --> 00:09:52,354
valid.

197
00:09:52,354 --> 00:09:53,553
Valid is freshness.

198
00:09:53,553 --> 00:09:56,204
Inactive is how long an unread item stays.

199
00:09:57,133 --> 00:09:59,234
Two lines that save you at three in the morning.

200
00:09:59,533 --> 00:09:59,794
One.

201
00:09:59,794 --> 00:10:01,103
proxy_cache_lock on.

202
00:10:01,103 --> 00:10:02,542
This is anti-stampede.

203
00:10:02,542 --> 00:10:07,840
When a popular object expires, only one request goes to the origin and the others

204
00:10:07,840 --> 00:10:08,167
wait.

205
00:10:08,167 --> 00:10:09,541
It is off by default.

206
00:10:09,866 --> 00:10:10,113
Two.

207
00:10:10,113 --> 00:10:11,472
proxy_cache_use_stale.

208
00:10:11,472 --> 00:10:16,599
Serve stale content rather than fail, when the origin errors, times out, or returns

209
00:10:16,599 --> 00:10:20,119
five hundred, five oh two, five oh three or five oh four.

210
00:10:20,119 --> 00:10:25,123
The updating option means one request refreshes the item while everyone else gets

211
00:10:25,123 --> 00:10:26,667
the stale copy instantly.

212
00:10:26,667 --> 00:10:28,581
That is stale-while-revalidate.

213
00:10:28,866 --> 00:10:33,422
And always add an X-Cache-Status header with the upstream cache status, so you can

214
00:10:33,422 --> 00:10:34,311
see miss or hit.

215
00:10:35,200 --> 00:10:36,547
location precedence.

216
00:10:36,547 --> 00:10:37,963
It is not file order.

217
00:10:39,000 --> 00:10:40,684
One: equals, exact match.

218
00:10:40,684 --> 00:10:43,446
It wins immediately and the search stops.

219
00:10:43,733 --> 00:10:45,952
Two: caret tilde, a prefix match.

220
00:10:45,952 --> 00:10:50,054
It takes the longest prefix, and suppresses the regex search.

221
00:10:50,054 --> 00:10:53,685
So/images/a dot php goes to number two, not the regex.

222
00:10:53,685 --> 00:10:55,703
Half the room gets this wrong.

223
00:10:56,000 --> 00:10:57,037
Three: a regex.

224
00:10:57,037 --> 00:11:00,632
The first match in file order wins, not the longest.

225
00:11:00,933 --> 00:11:05,263
Four: plain slash, the longest prefix, used only if no regex matched.

226
00:11:05,600 --> 00:11:10,339
So the algorithm is: exact match first, then the longest prefix, which is

227
00:11:10,339 --> 00:11:14,040
remembered, and if it is a caret tilde, the search stops.

228
00:11:14,040 --> 00:11:16,637
Then regexes in order, first match wins.

229
00:11:16,637 --> 00:11:18,974
Otherwise use the remembered prefix.

230
00:11:19,866 --> 00:11:23,941
Why are five hundred prefixes free, while five hundred regexes are not?

231
00:11:24,266 --> 00:11:29,374
Prefixes are stored in a ternary search tree, built at config parse time, and walked

232
00:11:29,374 --> 00:11:30,347
per URI segment.

233
00:11:30,347 --> 00:11:33,995
Regexes are a plain array, walked in order on every request.

234
00:11:33,995 --> 00:11:38,860
The data structure is the documentation: a tree means the longest wins, an array

235
00:11:38,860 --> 00:11:40,137
means the first wins.

236
00:11:40,466 --> 00:11:45,695
And nginx has no dot htaccess, deliberately, so it never has to stat its way up the

237
00:11:45,695 --> 00:11:47,711
directory tree on every request.

238
00:11:48,600 --> 00:11:52,326
Load balancing across upstreams: smooth weighted round robin.

239
00:11:52,326 --> 00:11:53,731
This is the core of it.

240
00:11:54,066 --> 00:11:57,755
Each round, every peer's current weight grows by its effective weight.

241
00:11:57,755 --> 00:12:00,178
Pick the peer with the highest current weight.

242
00:12:00,178 --> 00:12:02,286
Then subtract the total from the winner.

243
00:12:03,333 --> 00:12:07,066
Take weights three and one, so a is three and b is one.

244
00:12:07,066 --> 00:12:10,459
Round one: add, and the weights are three and one.

245
00:12:10,459 --> 00:12:10,934
Pick a.

246
00:12:10,934 --> 00:12:13,445
Subtract four from a: minus one, one.

247
00:12:13,733 --> 00:12:15,627
Round two: two and two.

248
00:12:15,627 --> 00:12:18,675
A tie, and the first peer wins, so a.

249
00:12:18,675 --> 00:12:20,734
Subtract: minus two, two.

250
00:12:21,066 --> 00:12:23,585
Round three: one and three.

251
00:12:23,585 --> 00:12:24,237
Pick b.

252
00:12:24,237 --> 00:12:26,569
Subtract: one, minus one.

253
00:12:26,866 --> 00:12:28,969
Round four: four and zero.

254
00:12:28,969 --> 00:12:29,535
Pick a.

255
00:12:29,535 --> 00:12:31,638
We are back to zero, zero.

256
00:12:31,933 --> 00:12:37,084
The ratio is the same three to one, but compare naive weighted round robin, which

257
00:12:37,084 --> 00:12:39,182
sends a, a, a, then b, in bursts.

258
00:12:39,182 --> 00:12:41,281
The smooth version interleaves b.

259
00:12:41,281 --> 00:12:44,016
The lecture writes the sequence as a b a a.

260
00:12:44,016 --> 00:12:47,132
Depending on how ties break, you may see a a b a.

261
00:12:47,132 --> 00:12:50,566
Either way, b is in the middle, not queued at the end.

262
00:12:50,566 --> 00:12:55,653
Measured over twenty-four requests, six went to one upstream and eighteen to the

263
00:12:55,653 --> 00:12:56,035
other.

264
00:12:56,035 --> 00:12:57,370
Exactly three to one.

265
00:12:57,370 --> 00:13:02,394
The other algorithm to try is least_conn: compare the p ninety-nine with a slow

266
00:13:02,394 --> 00:13:02,966
upstream.

267
00:13:03,866 --> 00:13:05,782
Fallbacks, with try_files.

268
00:13:06,066 --> 00:13:07,308
Three small blocks.

269
00:13:07,600 --> 00:13:08,993
Is this a real file?

270
00:13:09,333 --> 00:13:10,622
A real directory?

271
00:13:10,933 --> 00:13:11,130
No?

272
00:13:11,130 --> 00:13:12,704
Hand the URI to the app.

273
00:13:12,704 --> 00:13:14,737
The last entry is never tested.

274
00:13:14,737 --> 00:13:17,098
It is the fallback, not a candidate.

275
00:13:17,400 --> 00:13:22,996
A named location, with an at sign, is reachable only from try_files or error_page,

276
00:13:22,996 --> 00:13:24,157
never from a URL.

277
00:13:24,466 --> 00:13:29,958
WordPress, Rails, Django, and every single page app router are this one directive,

278
00:13:29,958 --> 00:13:32,570
plus an app that reads the request URI.

279
00:13:33,466 --> 00:13:34,898
Now a tour of the source.

280
00:13:34,898 --> 00:13:37,305
Line numbers drift, function names do not.

281
00:13:37,305 --> 00:13:41,658
Since nginx puts the return type on its own line, grep for the caret and the

282
00:13:41,658 --> 00:13:43,950
function name finds only the definition.

283
00:13:45,000 --> 00:13:52,095
src core is nginx's own standard library: strings, arrays, lists, hashes, red-black

284
00:13:52,095 --> 00:13:54,659
trees, queues, pools, buffers.

285
00:13:55,000 --> 00:13:59,899
src event holds the loop, and six ways to wait, chosen at configure time.

286
00:14:00,200 --> 00:14:06,422
src http holds the HTTP state machine, the phase engine and the upstream framework.

287
00:14:06,733 --> 00:14:09,415
src os unix is the syscall layer.

288
00:14:09,733 --> 00:14:15,615
src stream is the same architecture for raw TCP and UDP, which makes nginx a layer

289
00:14:15,615 --> 00:14:16,977
four load balancer.

290
00:14:17,266 --> 00:14:22,049
And src mail does the same for IMAP, POP3 and SMTP proxying.

291
00:14:22,333 --> 00:14:23,746
Two files to read first.

292
00:14:23,746 --> 00:14:28,513
ngx_queue.h is an intrusive doubly linked list: the link pointers live inside the

293
00:14:28,513 --> 00:14:30,397
node, so there is no allocation.

294
00:14:30,397 --> 00:14:33,281
The cache LRU and the posted event queues use it.

295
00:14:33,600 --> 00:14:35,581
ngx_palloc.h is the memory pool.

296
00:14:35,581 --> 00:14:40,782
Allocate from a pool attached to the request, and destroy the whole pool at the end.

297
00:14:40,782 --> 00:14:45,117
No reference counting, no class of leaks, and no free on the hot path.

298
00:14:46,066 --> 00:14:49,189
The HTTP parser is a resumable state machine.

299
00:14:49,533 --> 00:14:52,075
The request line alone has twenty-seven states.

300
00:14:55,600 --> 00:14:58,450
No regex, no strtok, no allocation.

301
00:14:58,450 --> 00:15:00,975
It consumes one byte at a time.

302
00:15:01,266 --> 00:15:03,350
It can stop between any two bytes.

303
00:15:03,350 --> 00:15:08,498
The state is saved, and parsing resumes where it stopped, because a request line may

304
00:15:08,498 --> 00:15:10,276
arrive in three TCP segments.

305
00:15:10,600 --> 00:15:15,000
This is what non-blocking costs you: every parser has to be resumable.

306
00:15:15,933 --> 00:15:18,324
Every request walks through eleven phases.

307
00:15:18,666 --> 00:15:25,681
Post read, server rewrite, find config, rewrite, post rewrite, pre-access, access,

308
00:15:25,681 --> 00:15:29,359
post access, pre-content, content, and log.

309
00:15:29,666 --> 00:15:32,237
The engine loops over the phase handlers.

310
00:15:32,237 --> 00:15:37,379
And here is the subtle part: when a checker returns NGX_OK, it does not mean done.

311
00:15:37,379 --> 00:15:38,570
It means suspended.

312
00:15:38,570 --> 00:15:40,890
This is a hand-rolled coroutine in C.

313
00:15:40,890 --> 00:15:44,840
The request resumes at the phase it left, when its event fires.

314
00:15:45,133 --> 00:15:50,871
Which module runs when: limit_req in pre-access, auth_basic in access, try_files in

315
00:15:50,871 --> 00:15:55,710
pre-content, and proxy_pass, fastcgi_pass and static files in content.

316
00:15:56,600 --> 00:15:59,966
Back to FastCGI, this time at the byte level.

317
00:16:00,266 --> 00:16:05,300
Every record has an eight byte header: version, type, request ID in two bytes,

318
00:16:05,300 --> 00:16:09,495
content length in two bytes, padding length, and a reserved byte.

319
00:16:09,495 --> 00:16:11,818
Then the content data, then padding.

320
00:16:12,133 --> 00:16:17,882
A begin request header is zero one, zero one, zero zero, zero one, zero zero, zero

321
00:16:17,882 --> 00:16:19,845
eight, zero zero, zero zero.

322
00:16:19,845 --> 00:16:24,051
Those are the first eight bytes of every FastCGI connection.

323
00:16:24,051 --> 00:16:29,660
And an empty params record is zero one, zero four, then the request, then length

324
00:16:29,660 --> 00:16:30,011
zero.

325
00:16:30,011 --> 00:16:32,044
That marks the end of params.

326
00:16:32,333 --> 00:16:34,187
An empty record is a delimiter.

327
00:16:34,187 --> 00:16:36,758
Forget it, and the responder waits forever.

328
00:16:36,758 --> 00:16:39,450
That is length plus delimiter, at two levels.

329
00:16:40,400 --> 00:16:45,353
The companion repository is a ladder of small programs, about twelve hundred lines,

330
00:16:45,353 --> 00:16:47,561
against nginx's two hundred thousand.

331
00:16:47,561 --> 00:16:49,351
The difference is error paths.

332
00:16:50,400 --> 00:16:53,372
Fork per client matches Apache one point three.

333
00:16:53,666 --> 00:16:58,444
A pthread per client matches the Apache two worker, and prints its eight megabyte

334
00:16:58,444 --> 00:16:58,798
stack.

335
00:16:59,133 --> 00:17:03,993
One thread with select dies at descriptor one thousand twenty-four, and ulimit does

336
00:17:03,993 --> 00:17:04,520
not help.

337
00:17:04,866 --> 00:17:07,097
One thread with epoll matches nginx.

338
00:17:07,097 --> 00:17:10,009
Five thousand connections, and nothing happens.

339
00:17:10,333 --> 00:17:15,604
read versus mmap versus sendfile: two thousand forty-eight system calls become one.

340
00:17:15,933 --> 00:17:19,903
The FastCGI responder talks the wire protocol at both ends.

341
00:17:20,200 --> 00:17:26,330
And one nginx conf, with five demos: routing, cache and weights, and fallbacks.

342
00:17:27,266 --> 00:17:28,857
Four ideas to leave with.

343
00:17:29,200 --> 00:17:29,435
One.

344
00:17:29,435 --> 00:17:30,792
Ask the other question.

345
00:17:30,792 --> 00:17:32,620
Apache made the worker cheaper.

346
00:17:32,620 --> 00:17:34,389
nginx asked why there was one.

347
00:17:34,733 --> 00:17:34,960
Two.

348
00:17:34,960 --> 00:17:37,350
Check whether the constraint still exists.

349
00:17:37,350 --> 00:17:41,219
Thread per connection died at eight megabytes plus context switches.

350
00:17:41,219 --> 00:17:43,893
Goroutines and virtual threads brought it back.

351
00:17:44,200 --> 00:17:44,629
Three.

352
00:17:44,629 --> 00:17:47,495
The data structure is the documentation.

353
00:17:47,495 --> 00:17:49,860
Prefixes in a tree: longest wins.

354
00:17:49,860 --> 00:17:52,152
Regexes in an array: first wins.

355
00:17:52,466 --> 00:17:52,873
Four.

356
00:17:52,873 --> 00:17:54,421
Amortise the setup.

357
00:17:54,421 --> 00:18:00,694
Prefork, pre-thread, servlets, connection pools, FastCGI, upstream keepalive.

358
00:18:00,694 --> 00:18:02,323
Six names, one move.

359
00:18:02,666 --> 00:18:05,846
The next question: where did all the state go?

360
00:18:05,846 --> 00:18:10,062
Sessions, sticky routing, TLS termination, and shared caches.

361
00:18:11,000 --> 00:18:14,413
Here is what the quiz is most likely to ask from session four.

362
00:18:38,466 --> 00:18:39,959
That is session four.

363
00:18:39,959 --> 00:18:45,502
Next up, session five: the evolution of HTTP, from nineteen ninety-six to now.

